# E0034: name defined twice

Functions, classes, enums and constants share one namespace. The fields and methods of a class share one, and so do the variants and methods of an enum. The labels of one variant's fields share one of their own, so two variants can use the same label. Each name is defined once in its namespace. The prelude functions `print`, `eprint`, `panic` and `read_line` are built in, so no function, class or constant can take their names.

## Why

A call or a type that names a duplicate could mean either definition. Sisu keeps the first and reports the second.

## Broken

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n + n
}

fn main() {
    print(double(2))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn main() {
    print(double(2))
}
```
